@page "/Home"

<MudText Class="mx-4 px-4 mt-4" Typo="Typo.h2">OSDC Trajectory</MudText>
<MudDivider Class="mb-4" />
<MudText Class="mx-6 px-6" Typo="Typo.body1">
    This microservice owns measured survey runs, calculated or imported trajectories, interpolation, aggregation,
    stochastic realization, extrapolation, target landing, directional-control evaluation, uncertainty, distance,
    and anti-collision calculations, and the shared identity and feature
    catalogs used to classify them. It persists to SQLite, enforces optimistic concurrency on every mutation, and
    exposes the same operations through a REST API, an MCP server, and a Blazor web application, so a survey run or
    trajectory created or edited in one interface is immediately visible through the others. Engineering values are
    stored in canonical SI units; depths and vertical coordinates are metres relative to WGS84.
    Creation and initial modification timestamps are assigned by the service for every persisted resource and
    calculation case; submitted timestamp values are not authoritative.
</MudText>

<MudGrid Class="mx-4 px-4 mt-2">
    <MudItem xs="12" md="6">
        <MudPaper Class="pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">What is a survey run</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                A <b>SurveyRun</b> is an imported measured survey dataset acquired with one survey instrument: a
                compact, editable list of <code>SurveyMeasurement</code> entries that preserves measured depth, original
                observed inclination/azimuth, their vertical and north references, optional measurement time in the
                selected time reference, and
                the applied correction with Earth-model provenance. Run-level defaults and an acquisition interval
                support historical files. Each run is tied to a <code>SurveyInstrumentID</code>, plus a tie-in point resolved from its
                <code>WellBoreID</code>, parent <code>WellID</code>, <code>ClusterID</code>, and
                <code>FieldID</code> chain, or from an upstream sidetrack point. Calculating a survey run turns the
                observations into WGS84-geodetic/true-north <code>SurveyStation</code> records—position, inclination, and azimuth—calling Earth Gravity and Earth Magnetic Field when required. Once an
                instrument error model is attached, calculation also produces an uncertainty covariance matrix using the minimum
                curvature method. A tied Wolff-de Wardt run replays its complete parent-run chain through the tie-in,
                including across a sidetrack that restarts measured depth, so its uncertainty ellipsoids remain continuous.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
                Every survey run can carry its own identity and feature assignments, drawn from the shared Trajectory
                catalogs, and large measurement or station lists use chunked read and write endpoints so that dense
                surveys transfer without oversized payloads.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mt-2">
                A run may optionally end at the bit rather than at the measurement tool. It can continue the final
                measured curve for a unit-aware tool-to-bit distance, or identify an existing final row as already
                extrapolated. The terminal station is labelled as extrapolated and is not processed as another
                instrument measurement.
            </MudText>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>
    <MudItem xs="12" md="6">
        <MudPaper Class="pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">What is a trajectory</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                A <b>Trajectory</b> is composed of one or more <code>TrajectorySurveyRunSection</code> entries,
                ordered by increasing start measured depth, each drawing its stations from a referenced survey run
                between its start abscissa and the next section's start (or the end of the well path for the last
                section). Calculating a trajectory materializes the combined <code>SurveyStationList</code> across
                all sections, so a single trajectory can represent a planned path spliced from several actual runs,
                a re-run replacing part of an earlier survey, or a sidetrack continuing from its parent. The materialized
                stations preserve the continued uncertainty calculated by their source survey runs.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
                Like survey runs, trajectories belong to a <code>WellBoreID</code> and its Well/Cluster/Field chain,
                carry identity and feature assignments, and hold the derived state — <code>TrajectoryType</code> and
                whether the trajectory is the current <b>definitive</b> one for its wellbore — that the anti-collision
                index relies on for filtering.
            </MudText>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>
</MudGrid>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Uncertainty calculation</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        Each survey station's position uncertainty is expressed as a covariance matrix built from the attached
        <b>SurveyInstrument</b> error model — <code>MWD_ISCWSA</code> / <code>Gyro_ISCWSA</code> (the ISCWSA weighting
        function method, summing correlated and uncorrelated error-source contributions along, across, and
        perpendicular to the wellbore) or <code>MWD_WolffDeWardt</code> / <code>Gyro_WolffDeWardt</code> (the simpler
        Wolff–de Wardt model). From that covariance, a <MudLink Href="/Trajectory/webapp/TrajectoryInterpolated">
        station-ellipse calculation</MudLink> derives horizontal, vertical, and perpendicular uncertainty ellipses at
        a caller-chosen <b>confidence factor</b> (95% by default), together with the highest- and lowest-TVD paths
        the true wellbore position could plausibly follow. Resource-owned ellipse views identify their SurveyRun or
        Trajectory so the service can replay complete parent ancestry and replace legacy or partial covariance before
        display; a partial Wolff–de Wardt list is never continued from covariance alone.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        The same covariance field underlies <b>trajectory realization</b>: a realization case draws a requested
        number of alternative wellbore paths from the uncertainty distribution around a reference trajectory
        (optionally coarsened first for performance), completes each with minimum curvature, and stores the results
        as chunked lists of realized survey points — useful for Monte-Carlo style visualization of where the actual
        wellbore could plausibly be. Realization generation uses the same lineage-aware rematerialization and fails
        closed when the reference trajectory's uncertainty ancestry cannot be reconstructed.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Aggregation and realization</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        <MudLink Href="/Trajectory/webapp/TrajectoryAggregation">Aggregation</MudLink> replaces dense source
        trajectories with a small, fitted chain of curve sections over
        common periods, reports the remaining approximation error, and samples the compact result with DLS, build
        rate, and turn rate. This is useful for creating a simpler planned reference without copying every original
        survey interval.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        <MudLink Href="/Trajectory/webapp/TrajectoryRealization">Trajectory Realizations</MudLink> draw alternative
        paths from the reference trajectory's reconstructed covariance field. Both workflows persist their cases;
        lightweight lists and calculation state remain small while large results are retrieved in chunks.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Directional control evaluation</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        The <MudLink Href="/Trajectory/webapp/DirectionalControlEvaluation">Directional Control Evaluation</MudLink>
        page compares an actual trajectory with the steering commands that reconnect-to-trajectory would have proposed
        toward a reference trajectory in the same WellBore. At each selected actual-MD interval it uses the first
        reconnect section and fits the observed response with the same circular-arc, build/turn, or curvature/toolface model.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        Residual command pairs are bundled jointly over depth, while invalid evaluations may be bridged only up to the
        configured hard gap. Each bundle reports empirical P10, P50 and P90 together with robust distribution statistics.
        The calculation runs durably in the service; case discovery and progress remain lightweight, and detailed interval
        samples are retrieved in zero-based chunks. No command delay, lead-in, or wellbore-position uncertainty is assumed.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Interpolation</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        An <MudLink Href="/Trajectory/webapp/TrajectoryInterpolated">interpolated trajectory</MudLink> recomputes a
        source trajectory's path at a denser or differently spaced set of measured depths using the minimum
        curvature method, the same geometric method used to turn raw MD/inclination/azimuth survey measurements into
        3D positions. Spacing can be driven either by a fixed <code>InterpolationStep</code> along a chosen
        <code>InterpolationReferenceDepth</code>, or adaptively by a <code>MaximumChordArcDistance</code> tolerance
        that inserts extra stations only where the wellbore curves enough for the straight chord between two stations
        to depart from the true arc by more than the allowed distance.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        Interpolation can also be forced at specific abscissas — casing and liner shoe depths, liner hanger depths,
        casing diameter changes, or any caller-supplied annotated depth — and can optionally include the source
        trajectory's first and last surveys, so an interpolated trajectory lines up with the engineering depths that
        other services (WellBore Architecture, drilling reports) care about.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Distance between trajectory axes</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        <b>Survey run</b> and <b>trajectory minimum-distance calculations</b> find the closest approach between a
        reference wellbore's centerline and one or more comparison centerlines: each trajectory is broken into
        straight-line segments between consecutive stations, the comparison segments are indexed in a local octree
        (bounded by a configurable maximum depth and maximum segment count per leaf) so that only nearby segments are
        tested, and the true closest-approach distance is then solved segment-by-segment. The calculation can
        optionally account for borehole radius, subtracting each wellbore's own diameter from the raw centerline
        distance.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        An <b>adaptive refinement</b> setting lets the search increase resolution automatically wherever two
        trajectories pass unusually close, instead of applying uniform, possibly wasteful, mesh density everywhere.
        Results are reported per reference interval and, for the closest points overall, as a chunked, sortable
        result set so dense comparisons against many offset wells stay usable.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Safety factors between uncertainty volumes</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        Centerline distance alone does not say whether two wells are actually at risk of colliding — the
        <b>separation factor</b> (also known as the ISCWSA collision-risk ratio, or pedal-curve method) does. At each
        point along the reference trajectory, the uncertainty ellipsoids of the reference and comparison wellbores
        — built from their covariance matrices at the chosen confidence factor — are scaled together, uniformly, until
        their surfaces first touch. The scale factor at which that happens is the separation factor: a value below 1
        means the (unscaled) ellipsoids already overlap at the requested confidence level, while a value above 1
        expresses how much headroom exists before they would.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        Because the comparison wellbore is normally surveyed less densely than the reference, the calculation
        automatically increases the number of interpolated ellipses on the comparison side — driven by the minimum
        MD spacing on the reference and the maximum MD spacing on the comparison — before intersecting the two
        envelopes, so the result does not depend on which of the two trajectories happens to carry more survey
        stations.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mt-2">
        The <MudLink Href="/Trajectory/webapp/AntiCollisionScan">Anti-collision Scan</MudLink> page selects a
        reference through searchable Field/Cluster/Well/WellBore/Trajectory selectors that match any part of a name,
        finds overlapping planned or actual trajectory
        envelopes through the global octree, and calculates separation-factor tables and interactive depth profiles
        only for the selected candidates and relevant measured-depth intervals. Both octree discovery and
        separation-factor work run as background service jobs; the page shows measured stage progress and retrieves
        each result only when its job completes, so scans lasting several minutes do not depend on one long HTTP
        request. Separation-factor confidence is selected through the shared Unit Reference system, defaults to
        95%, and cannot exceed the octree encoding confidence of 99.9%. The profile depth axis can fit the union of
        calculated intervals or show the complete reference trajectory.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mt-2">
        The <MudLink Href="/Trajectory/webapp/AntiCollisionPolicies">Anti-collision Policies</MudLink> page maintains
        immutable policy revisions and non-overlapping Field assignments. Assignment dates are presented in the
        selected time reference. Rules use first-match priority with
        comparison-side age, identity, and feature conditions, plus one unconditional default. Each rule defines a
        dimensionless Alert threshold above its Alarm threshold. A completed calculation freezes the exact policy,
        assignment, evaluation time, evidence, thresholds, and per-comparison classification; missing evidence is
        reported as indeterminate instead of silently selecting a less conservative rule.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Octree encoding and automatic anti-collision scanning</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        Running a full minimum-distance or separation-factor calculation against every other trajectory in the
        database for every edit would not scale. Instead, the service maintains a persistent, whole-Earth
        <b>global anti-collision octree index</b>: each trajectory's 99.9%-confidence uncertainty volume is enclosed
        by a one-cell-padded union of short, solid, axis-aligned boxes swept between successive uncertainty ellipses.
        The conservative cover is encoded at a detailed depth of 22 — approximately 4.5&nbsp;m north-south,
        9.0&nbsp;m east-west at the equator (about 5.0&nbsp;m near Valhall), and 9.54&nbsp;m vertically. Filling the volume and its ends prevents strict
        containment or end entry from being missed; box corners may add candidates that the separation-factor
        calculation subsequently rejects. Membership is stored as one row per
        <code>(octree depth, coarse code, trajectory UUID)</code>, so a spatial query first narrows candidates by
        coarse bucket, joins the derived <code>TrajectoryType</code> / <code>IsDefinitive</code> state for filtering,
        and only then performs exact intersection on the detailed codes.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        This index is <b>derived, not authoritative</b>: it is rebuilt automatically whenever a trajectory is
        calculated, updated, deleted, or restored, and a background reconciliation pass on startup repairs entries
        that are missing, outdated, or orphaned. The REST/MCP status operation reports, per trajectory, whether its
        cached index is current, stale, or missing, along with its schema version, confidence factor, calculation
        hash, bucket count, and detailed-code count — so callers can trust automatic anti-collision scanning without
        needing to trigger it by hand.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Trajectory extrapolation</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        The <MudLink Href="/Trajectory/webapp/TrajectoryExtrapolation">Trajectory Extrapolation</MudLink> page
        starts from the last calculated station of a source trajectory. It can extend a selected length in a straight
        line or by continuing the last fitted curve, reconnect to an advanced point and tangent on another trajectory
        with a two-section solver, solve a geosteering target from overall departure/bearing or steering length,
        or solve a user-defined well path made from circular-arc, constant-build-and-turn, and constant-curvature-and-toolface sections.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        Reconnect and geosteering calculations may first continue the source trajectory's current curve for a lead-in;
        overall geosteering departure remains referenced to the original final source station, while steering length
        covers only the two steering sections and excludes the lead-in.
        The well-path table accepts exactly three constraints per section on average, prevents overdetermined prefixes,
        and keeps incompatible curve parameter pairs separate. Calculations run in the service, are polled through a
        lightweight status, and persist frozen endpoint/revision metadata plus chunked survey stations. Lengths are
        stored in metres, angles in radians, and curvature/rates in radians per metre; the page applies the selected
        display units without changing those canonical values. The interpolated result can be exported using the same
        ASCII structure as calculated Survey Runs and Trajectories, or saved atomically as a planned Survey Run in the
        source WellBore after entering its name; the last usable source Survey Run supplies its survey instrument.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Target landing</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        The <MudLink Href="/Trajectory/webapp/TargetLanding">Target Landing</MudLink> page designs a landing from
        the final station of a calculated trajectory into a convex polygon on an oriented plane. The plane origin can
        be entered as Riemannian North/East or WGS84 latitude/longitude and its normal as inclination and azimuth.
        Free-attitude cases use one landing section; <i>Land perpendicular to target plane</i> uses the corresponding
        two-section circular-arc, build/turn, or curvature/toolface solver.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        Driller targets use the specified polygon directly. Geological targets shrink it to the sampled region where
        the complete projected position-uncertainty ellipse remains inside the boundary at the requested confidence.
        A separate reachable zone applies the optional Maximum Landing Curvature—3°/30 m by default—only to the newly
        designed landing sections. The service stores the adaptive mesh, contours and shortest forward solutions,
        preserves Wolff-de Wardt uncertainty through the lead and landing, and marks results stale when the source or
        case input changes.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Hierarchy and rig-job selection</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        Survey Run, Trajectory, Extrapolation, Target Landing, and Anti-collision pages use the same searchable Field, Cluster, Well,
        WellBore, and resource selector. Each box accepts partial names. Rig job is selected separately because one
        WellBore can have several dated rig jobs. The job matching the Survey Run acquisition date—or the latest
        constituent Survey Run date for a Trajectory—is selected by default. Changing it updates the rotary-table
        depth used by depth-reference conversion without modifying the stored survey or trajectory.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Identities and features</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        Every survey run and trajectory can carry any number of user-defined <b>identities</b> — symbolic names such
        as a planning name, a company- or regulatory-reporting name, or a nickname — and any number of <b>feature</b>
        assignments drawn from user-defined categories and options, optionally exclusive within a category and
        optionally time-bounded. The default identities are <code>NameForPlanning</code>,
        <code>NameForCompanyReporting</code>, <code>NameForRegulatoryReporting</code>, <code>Nickname</code>, and
        <code>NameForOperationReporting</code>; default feature categories cover survey and borehole-section context,
        survey and trajectory purpose, reference status, acquisition mode, measurement condition, running mode, data
        processing state, correction applied, quality status/issue, and survey station density.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        The catalogs themselves are managed on the <MudLink Href="/Trajectory/webapp/TrajectoryIdentities">
        Identities</MudLink> and <MudLink Href="/Trajectory/webapp/TrajectoryFeatures">Features</MudLink> pages,
        shared between survey runs and trajectories; a definition or option cannot be deleted while still referenced
        by a survey run or trajectory.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Contextual data and calculators</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        The <b>Survey Display</b> menu shows survey runs and trajectories grouped by their WellBore, Well, Cluster,
        or Field, so a set of paths can be reviewed in its drilling-program context without leaving this application.
        Position-reference choices include Field-relative coordinates and a cartographic grid offset calculated from
        the owning Field through its coordinate-conversion API; unavailable derived references fall back to WGS84.
        The <b>Contextual Data</b> menu gives read access to the underlying <MudLink Href="/Trajectory/webapp/Field">
        Field</MudLink>, <MudLink Href="/Trajectory/webapp/Cluster">Cluster</MudLink>,
        <MudLink Href="/Trajectory/webapp/Well">Well</MudLink>,
        <MudLink Href="/Trajectory/webapp/WellBore">WellBore</MudLink>,
        <MudLink Href="/Trajectory/webapp/Rig">Rig</MudLink>, and
        <MudLink Href="/Trajectory/webapp/SurveyInstrument">Survey Instrument</MudLink> records those trajectories
        reference, each backed by its own microservice and shown here through reusable Razor components.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        The <b>Calculators</b> menu exposes the same synchronous, stateless evaluations used elsewhere in OSDC —
        <MudLink Href="/Trajectory/webapp/FieldCartographicConverter">Cartographic Conversion</MudLink>,
        <MudLink Href="/Trajectory/webapp/VerticalDatum">Vertical Datum Conversion</MudLink>,
        <MudLink Href="/Trajectory/webapp/GravitationalField">Earth Gravity Evaluation</MudLink>, and
        <MudLink Href="/Trajectory/webapp/EarthMagneticField">Earth Magnetic Field Evaluation</MudLink> — for one-off
        conversions and lookups while working a survey run or trajectory, without switching to another site.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Backup and restore</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        Backups are logical, portable JSON, not a raw database copy. From the
        <MudLink Href="/Trajectory/webapp/TrajectoryBackupRestore">Backup / Restore</MudLink> page, export one or
        many survey runs and trajectories together into one versioned document. Selecting a trajectory automatically
        includes the survey runs it references and their parent chain, so a selection is never missing the data
        needed to recalculate it; survey runs can also be selected independently of any trajectory. Backup schema
        version 2 also carries the complete immutable anti-collision policy library and Field assignment history.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        Restoring validates the complete dependency graph, resolves the shared identity/feature catalogs — matching
        catalog UUIDs exactly by default, with normalized-name mapping available only as an explicit opt-in — validates
        policy revisions and non-overlapping assignment intervals, writes survey runs before the trajectories
        that reference them, and commits every record in one transaction without
        recalculation, so a restore of many survey runs and trajectories either succeeds completely or leaves the
        database untouched. <MudLink Href="/Trajectory/webapp/SurveyRunBatchImport">Batch Import</MudLink> offers a
        separate, simpler path for bulk-loading external survey measurements as new survey runs.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Data integrity and monitoring</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        Survey run and trajectory updates and deletes use <b>optimistic concurrency</b>: every mutation must echo
        back the <code>LastModificationDate</code> from the caller's latest read, and a stale request is rejected
        rather than silently overwriting a concurrent change. Persisted calculation cases and results — interpolated
        and realized trajectories, station ellipses, minimum-distance and separation-factor results — are durable and
        are never removed automatically, however old; deletion only happens through an explicit delete operation.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        Because <code>FieldID</code>, <code>ClusterID</code>, <code>WellID</code>, <code>WellBoreID</code>, and a
        survey run's <code>SurveyInstrumentID</code> point at other services, Trajectory also offers read-only
        <b>external-reference validation</b> for one record and a bounded <b>audit</b> across stored records, each
        reference reported as <code>Valid</code>, <code>Invalid</code>, or <code>Unavailable</code> so a dependency
        outage is never confused with a genuinely broken reference. The
        <MudLink Href="/Trajectory/webapp/StatisticsTrajectory">Usage Statistics</MudLink> page reports per-endpoint
        request counts for this service replica.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4 mb-6" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Access methods</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-3">
        The same survey run and trajectory data is reachable through several interfaces built on the same underlying
        store, so it can be managed interactively, scripted, embedded in other Blazor applications, or wired into AI
        agents.
    </MudText>
    <MudSimpleTable>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Method</th><th>Description</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><b>Web page</b></td>
                <td>
                    This site — a server-side Blazor application (this page) for browsing, editing, interpolating,
                    realizing, aggregating, comparing, backing up, and restoring survey runs and trajectories,
                    inspecting contextual data, running calculators, and reviewing usage statistics.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><b>REST API</b></td>
                <td>
                    JSON endpoints under <code>/Trajectory/api</code> for survey-run and trajectory CRUD, chunked
                    measurement/station transfer, batch import, identity and feature catalog management, interpolation,
                    realization, aggregation, station ellipses, minimum distance, extrapolation, target landing,
                    directional-control evaluation, asynchronous octree candidate scans, durable separation-factor
                    jobs, immutable policies/Field assignments, and octree status/repair operations,
                    external-reference validation/audit, and batch export/restore, documented and explorable through
                    the Swagger UI at <code>/Trajectory/api/swagger</code>. Suited to scripts, backend integration,
                    and any HTTP client.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><b>MCP</b></td>
                <td>
                    A Model Context Protocol server exposed as Streamable HTTP at <code>/Trajectory/api/mcp</code>
                    (and WebSocket at <code>/Trajectory/api/mcp/ws</code>), publishing 176 REST-backed tools plus
                    connectivity <code>ping</code>. The two unrestricted primary-resource list operations are omitted
                    in favor of bounded search tools. Strict, closed input/output JSON Schemas guide agents through
                    survey/trajectory management and every calculation family, including queued status/result and
                    zero-based chunk workflows;
                    invalid filter combinations, comparison sets, reference choices, and caller-supplied derived
                    calculation fields are rejected before work is queued.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><b>Reusable web pages</b></td>
                <td>
                    The Razor components behind the survey-run, trajectory, catalog, calculation, and backup/restore
                    pages are published as the <code>OSDC.Drilling.Trajectory.WebPages</code> NuGet package, so other
                    Blazor applications — such as the Well or WellBore web apps — can embed the same unit-aware
                    controls instead of re-implementing this UI.
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </MudSimpleTable>
</MudPaper>
